<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 900px; margin: auto;">

    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # <span style="font-weight: normal;">Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You.</span> – Lesson # 1</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Planning Meals for My Family</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow narratives involving familiar contexts. Interactive: Respond to questions on familiar and routine matters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow narratives related to types of food (e.g., "Vegetables and grains are healthy."). Can respond to questions about healthy eating (e.g., "What types of food are good for you?" "Fruits and vegetables are good choices."). Can summarize information about meal planning shared in class (e.g., "Use a chart or a Venn diagram to present the main ideas shared in class.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 20px; margin-top: 25px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or realia of various fruits (e.g., mangos, watermelon) and vegetables (e.g., tomatoes, lettuce). Students listen and repeat the names. The teacher asks simple questions like, "What is this?" and "Do you like mangos?" to activate prior knowledge related to the theme "Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You." and introduce core vocabulary such as *fruits, vegetables, mangos, watermelon, tomatoes, lettuce*.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary, focusing on /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit" by clearly articulating the sounds. The teacher says phrases like, "Fruits are healthy." and "Vegetables are good for you." The teacher models asking, "What do you like to eat?" and answering, "I like to eat some mangos."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary and concepts.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a mango a fruit or a vegetable?" (Expected answer: "A fruit.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are French fries healthy?" (Expected answer: "No, they are not healthy.")</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio narrative (1-1.5 minutes) about a child and parent discussing what they can *cook* and *eat* for different *meals* (e.g., *breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack*). The narrative includes items like *rice, beans, water, juice*, and mentions *healthy* food choices. The audio should explicitly use the present simple and modal 'can' (e.g., "We can cook rice and beans for lunch. We enjoy drinking juice with our meals.").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will follow a simple narrative involving familiar contexts (meal planning) and understand key information about types of food and meal times. This builds on "Listening: Receptive: Follow narratives involving familiar contexts."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "Who is talking in the audio?" (Expected answer: "A child and a parent.") "What are they talking about?" (Expected answer: "Food / meals.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen and mark statements as True or False based on the audio. E.g., "They eat pizza for dinner." (F) "They can drink water." (T)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match food items mentioned to the meal times (e.g., "rice and beans" to "lunch," "mangos" to "snack").</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific details using present simple, modal verbs ('can'), countable/uncountable nouns ('some rice', 'many mangos'), and associating vocabulary related to food and meals (*breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, rice, beans, water, juice, fruits, vegetables, healthy, tasty*).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher plays short sentences from the audio narrative again, pausing at key points. Students listen and complete sentence frames on a worksheet with target vocabulary. E.g., "For _____, we can eat _____ and _____." (Expected answer: "dinner, rice, beans"). This reinforces listening for specific words and understanding their context.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Listen and Identify):</b> The teacher reads aloud short descriptions of different meals or snacks using vocabulary like *tasty, salty, sour, sweet, healthy*. Students listen and identify if the food described is "healthy" or "not healthy" by circling or pointing to an emoji. E.g., Teacher says: "I like to eat some sweet watermelon. It is very fresh." Students circle "healthy." Teacher says: "I often eat salty chips." Students circle "not healthy."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listen and Confirm):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A makes a sentence about a food preference using "I like to eat some [countable/uncountable noun] for [meal time]." (e.g., "I like to eat some bread for breakfast.") Student B listens carefully and repeats the sentence, then asks, "Is that true?" Student A confirms "Yes, that's true" or "No, I like to eat some mangos for snack." This allows students to practice both active listening and short responses to familiar matters.</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative listening and visual meal planning. Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher plays a slightly longer audio (2-3 minutes) of a family planning their meals for one day (breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack), explicitly mentioning types of food, quantities (e.g., "some rice", "many fruits"), and health aspects. The audio uses sequencing adverbs like "first," "next."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen carefully to the audio to understand the family's meal plan for the day.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening twice, each group collaboratively creates a simple visual meal plan (e.g., drawing pictures or writing lists on a chart divided into "Breakfast," "Lunch," "Dinner," "Snack") that reflects what they heard. They should focus on incorporating *fruits and vegetables* as good choices, as per the theme.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups discuss and summarize the main ideas from the audio. E.g., "For breakfast, they eat eggs and some bread."</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly applies the learned vocabulary and listening skills to the scenario of "Planning Meals for My Family" while reinforcing the theme "Fruits and Vegetables Are Good for You." Students actively listen to plan healthy meals.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student reflection.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group work and a quick peer assessment during the presentation.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their visual meal plan (the one they created based on the audio) to the class. One member from each group shares one meal, e.g., "For breakfast, the family can eat mangos and bread." The teacher asks follow-up questions to the group, such as "What types of food are good for you?" or "What fruits do they enjoy for snack?" This assesses their ability to respond to questions on familiar and routine matters and summarize information shared.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit" and other key vocabulary.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., *breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, fruits, vegetables, healthy*).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in responding to simple questions about the meal plan.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative effort within the group to create the meal plan.</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I understood most of the audio about healthy meals: Yes / Sometimes / No."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can name three healthy foods I heard today." (Students write three words.)</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me to understand when listening today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to the meal plan was difficult for me?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the lesson worked well for the students in terms of listening comprehension and vocabulary acquisition? (e.g., "The flashcards and initial modeling of /v/ and /f/ were effective. Students successfully identified meal times."). What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with following the narrative or responding to questions? (e.g., "Need simpler audio for some students, or more visual support during listening tasks for identifying healthy/unhealthy food.").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The vocabulary (fruits, vegetables, meal times, healthy food) and basic understanding of meal planning established in this listening lesson will be directly applied in Lesson 2, which will focus on reading comprehension of a text about healthy eating habits, using the same lexical set.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>